  {
   "title": "desayunar = to have breakfast",
   "body": "Literally 'un-fast' (des = un, ayunar = to fast), just like English break-fast. El desayuno = breakfast.",
   "examples": [
    {
     "en": "I'm having breakfast.",
     "es": "Desayuno."
    }
   ],
   "t": 56.1
  },
  {
   "title": "Present = 'I speak' and 'I'm speaking'",
   "body": "English overuses -ing, even for the future ('he's doing it tomorrow'). Spanish uses the plain present there: lo hace mañana. Spanish -ing exists, but only for what's happening right now.",
   "t": 140.1
  },
  {
   "title": "estar + -ando = right now",
   "body": "What you're doing is a state, so it's always estar. For -ar verbs, drop -r … add -ando: hablando.",
   "examples": [
    {
     "en": "I'm speaking (right now).",
     "es": "Estoy hablando."
    },
    {
     "en": "I'm waiting here.",
     "es": "Estoy esperando aquí."
    }
   ],
   "caveat": "Never for the future: not 'estoy comiendo con ella mañana'.",
   "t": 202.1
  },
  {
   "title": "Future plan vs right now",
   "body": "Lo can go before estoy or attach to the -ando form (which then takes an accent).",
   "examples": [
    {
     "en": "I'm preparing it tomorrow / later.",
     "es": "Lo preparo mañana. / Lo preparo más tarde."
    },
    {
     "en": "I'm preparing it (right now).",
     "es": "Lo estoy preparando. / Estoy preparándolo."
    },
    {
     "en": "We're having breakfast (now).",
     "es": "Estamos desayunando."
    }
   ],
   "t": 313.1
  },
  {
   "title": "-er / -ir → -iendo",
   "body": "",
   "examples": [
    {
     "en": "I'm eating (right now).",
     "es": "Estoy comiendo."
    },
    {
     "en": "He's eating (can't come to the phone).",
     "es": "Está comiendo."
    },
    {
     "en": "They're selling it (right now).",
     "es": "Lo están vendiendo. / Están vendiéndolo."
    },
    {
     "en": "They're selling it tomorrow.",
     "es": "Lo venden mañana."
    }
   ],
   "t": 460.2
  }
